outputs.L1:
A capsule, particularly suited for preparing a beverage from beverage powder—such as coffee from coffee powder—by introducing water into the capsule, is disclosed. The capsule comprises a compacted pellet formed from a powder containing at least one polysaccharide, wherein said pellet is sheathed with at least one coating layer. This coating layer comprises a cross-linked polysaccharide, obtainable by cross-linking a polysaccharide with a cross-linking agent, without recourse to a polyol spacer.

A method for manufacturing such a capsule is also provided, comprising the following steps:

i) preparing a compacted pellet from a powder containing at least one polysaccharide;

ii) contacting at least a portion—preferably the entire surface—of the compacted pellet obtained in step i) with either a solution of a polysaccharide in a solvent or a dispersion of a polysaccharide in a dispersant;

iii) optionally, removing the compacted pellet from the solution or dispersion obtained in step ii);

iv) contacting the compacted pellet obtained in step ii) or iii) with at least one cross-linking agent;

v) optionally, removing the compacted pellet from the solution obtained in step iv); and

vi) drying the compacted pellet obtained in step iv) or v).

outputs.L2:
A capsule designed for making beverages from powdered ingredients—coffee being a prime example—works by allowing water to flow into it. This capsule contains a pressed pellet formed from powder that includes at least one type of polysaccharide. This pellet is covered with a coating consisting of a cross-linked polysaccharide. Notably, this cross-linked polysaccharide is produced by treating a polysaccharide with a cross-linking agent, and this process does not require any polyol spacer.

The process for producing such a capsule involves the following stages:

i) Forming a compressed pellet using a polysaccharide-containing powder;

ii) Exposing at least part of this pellet—ideally its entire surface—to either a polysaccharide solution or a polysaccharide dispersion, using an appropriate solvent or dispersing medium;

iii) If needed, extracting the pellet from this solution or dispersion;

iv) Treating the pellet (resulting from step ii or iii) with a cross-linking agent;

v) If required, taking the pellet out of the solution used in step iv;

vi) Finally, allowing the pellet (from step iv or v) to dry completely.
